# Cloudflare Pages applies these only when no static asset matches, so a
# PUBLISHED feed never reaches them. Most specific first.
# Record and corrections became one page. Both old paths are published, linked
# from a partner placement, and indexed, so they move rather than break.
/record/               /proof/                301
/record                /proof/                301
/corrections/          /proof/#corrections    301
/corrections           /proof/#corrections    301
# A PLACEHOLDER, not a splat. Cloudflare Pages only honours `*` as a TRAILING
# splat, so `/archive/*/feed.json` never matched anything and neither did
# `/*.json`: both fell through to the HTML 404 below. The monitor found it the
# first day it got far enough to look, and it means a partner asking for a feed
# on an unpublished date has always been handed a web page where the contract
# promises a JSON error body. `/feed-not-found.json` has been published and
# unreachable this whole time.
/archive/:slug/feed.json   /feed-not-found.json   404
# `/*.json` has no working equivalent here: a splat before a literal suffix is
# the exact form Pages does not support, and there is no content negotiation in
# this file. The rule above covers the path a partner actually requests; every
# other JSON route on the site is a real file. Rather than leave a rule that
# looks like a safety net and is not one, the general case is stated as
# unhandled.
/*                         /404.html              404
